#!/usr/bin/env python3
# vendi-phone-hid — this computer becomes a Bluetooth keyboard + mouse for
# your iPhone, driven by the phone mirror window (`vendi phone control`).
#
# vendiwm decides what goes to the phone: clicks/drags inside the iPhone
# mirror, the wheel over it, and keys typed while it has focus. They arrive
# here over vendiwm's IPC ("phone" events) and leave as Bluetooth HID reports.
#
# iOS only takes a RELATIVE mouse (AssistiveTouch pointer), so every tap is
# made absolute: slam the pointer into the top-left corner (it stops at the
# edge, so its position is known), then move to the target at a constant
# speed — constant speed keeps iOS's pointer acceleration linear, so one
# calibration (`vendi phone calibrate`: click where the pointer landed) maps
# mirror coordinates to pointer movement. Calibration also probes whether iOS
# accepts an absolute pointer; if it does, taps are exact with no reset.
#
# Runs as root (HID's L2CAP ports need it). While it runs, bluetoothd runs
# without its `input` plugin (which otherwise owns those ports); put back on
# exit, even on a crash.

